---
import config from '../config.json';
import EventRow from './EventRow.astro';
import PhotoSlot from './PhotoSlot.astro';
import {
  content, upcoming, value, filled, copiedOn, formatDate, ageSince, builtOn,
  scheduleConfigured,
} from '../lib/content.ts';
// The window rules live outside this component so the gate that reads the built page (`scripts/check-schedule-window.mjs`)
// can compare it against the same rule rather than a copy of it (D238).
import { todayIn } from '../lib/events.mjs';
import { HORIZON_WEEKS, MAX_ROWS, scheduleWindow } from '../lib/event-window.mjs';

/**
 * When we meet — the schedule, in two widths of one component.
 *
 * What it replaced, and why the replacement had to be written rather than enabled:
 * `.notes/roadmap-events.md` measured `UpcomingMeetups.astro` as "**entirely invented placeholder
 * content**" — "Saturday, 2:00 PM", "Local Library - Study Room B", "Topic: Introduction to Group
 * Theory", a "Reserve Your Spot" button — and said any schedule work should **replace** it, not turn it
 * on. It was also pre-design CSS (`bg-blue-50`, `text-gray-900`, `shadow-lg`) and it pointed at
 * `/math-study-session.jpg`, a file that is not in `public/`, so switching the flag on would have both
 * published fiction about a real club and failed the build. `PastEvents.astro` was `<h2>Past Events</h2>`
 * and a comment. Both flags were `false` in `src/config.json`, and prod agrees — the curl numbers are in
 * the roadmap note.
 *
 * The three states, all designed, none of them an invented event (record 0010: missing content is a state,
 * never a blank, never a build failure; DESIGN.md "States"):
 *   - **dates exist** → rows: date lane, series name, status word. D92: the site publishes **series names
 *     and real dates**, never a frequency.
 *   - **a source is configured and holds nothing** → the round-7 empty state the operator chose (option A as
 *     measured then: 305px, status word + title + two-line body + a 48px primary and a 40px secondary — D139
 *     has since retired the 40px step for buttons — + the dated line, "two paths out"), because a guaranteed
 *     condition of an upcoming-only feed is a gap between events.
 *   - **no source configured yet** (D132 rules the source is Mobilizon or an ICS feed, and the committed
 *     snapshot says it was read from one) → the same block, with its own status line, and the bracketed
 *     `[venue blurb]` from `src/content.json` **not rendered**: a bracket is a hole in our knowledge, and
 *     DESIGN.md keeps brackets off the page until a source exists. `scripts/check-content.mjs` fails a
 *     build that ships one.
 *
 * Controls, after the D139 ruling (2026-11-03):
 *   - One control per band: the **48px primary** in the hero's own grammar (`px-8 py-4`, no hard-coded
 *     height — DESIGN.md: "heights are derived, not chosen"), declaring `data-control="primary"` because the
 *     height rules now match the name the source publishes (D140).
 *   - Every other path is an **inline link, not a second button.** It was written while the gate still demanded
 *     a 40px secondary and then forbade one (record 0016); the ruling retired 40px for buttons, so a second
 *     button would now be legal at 48px. It stays a link anyway: DESIGN.md prices an in-sentence path at its
 *     24px line, and a notice-shaped block reads better with one pressable block and one path in prose than
 *     with two buttons of the same size.
 * Every path goes somewhere that exists — D117's rule is that a path needs a page (that is what killed
 * `Schedule` and `Members` from the nav). As of 2026-11-07 the `Schedule` path has a page:
 * `src/pages/schedule.astro`, so the nav label is back with something behind it.
 *
 * 2026-11-07, the split (D227). The homepage used to print up to four rows, which made the landing page a
 * calendar and left the calendar with no home: `/events.ics` existed, `/schedule` did not, and a visitor who
 * wanted "what else is coming?" had one answer only — paste a URL into a calendar client. Now:
 *   - `variant="full"` (the `/schedule` page) lists every open date in the snapshot — **within a window**
 *     (D238). The "no cap" this line used to assert was true for one day: an uncapped list is a page that grows
 *     with every date a host creates, and D224 priced one art-bearing row at **+197px of page**. What this
 *     variant lists is `HORIZON_WEEKS` weeks ahead, at most `MAX_ROWS` rows, both from
 *     `src/lib/event-window.mjs`, and the page says out loud what it left out. `/events.ics` stays uncapped:
 *     the limit is a page-layout rule, not a fact about the club's calendar.
 *   - `variant="compact"` (the homepage, `src/pages/index.astro`) prints **no date at all** and points at the page
 *     of record. Since 2026-11-14 that is the operator's rule, not a layout choice: the `/join` objective says
 *     "`/schedule` stays the only surface that prints dates", and `check:dated-surfaces` enforces it on every
 *     built page (D306 supersedes the date half of D227; the join mechanics D228 absorbed here stay).
 *   - `limit` is the prop that says how many rows, so the two call sites read the same `upcoming()` and
 *     cannot drift into two calendars. `check:ics` and `check:content` are what keep the page and
 *     `/events.ics` honest about the dates themselves.
 *
 * 2026-11-07, the second half of the same change (D228): the compact width **absorbed the join band**
 * (`JoinCommunity.astro`, "Come once / How it works"), which is deleted. Why merged rather than left beside
 * it: the two bands answered one question in two places — one said "here is a date", the other said "here is
 * how you come to a date" — with the horizon band between them, so a reader met the promise and its mechanics
 * three screens apart. The landing page now runs hero → what we take up → the horizon → **come once, with the
 * next date inside it** → footer. That is a rhythm change and it has not been priced: `npm run check:rhythm`
 * owes it a number before anyone claims one (asked in `.notes/NEXT.md` with A3).
 *
 * The compact card is deliberately NOT the full width's row. One row of list markup would read as a
 * truncated list, which is what the old homepage was; the card draws the date the way a calendar draws a
 * date — month, day number, weekday, in a filled leaf — and the leaf is the sign that this surface is a
 * preview of a page rather than a short list. The full width's rows stay ruled strips (D220's `edges`),
 * because there the dates, not one date, are the thing.
 *
 * The rhythm inside the full width is R6's `rows` option, ruled 2026-11-05 (D214; the pair's measurement is
 * D220): 24px below the heading (was 48), 16px between rows (was 24), 8px of row padding (was 12). Round 15
 * priced the option at **-88px of page at 1440/1240/1024 and -120px at 640/390/320, with the emptiness
 * falling 36-73px** @measured 2026-11-04 by `npm run check:rhythm`; as shipped with `edges`, this section
 * alone measured **80px shorter at 1240** (852 -> 772) @measured 2026-11-05 by `npm run check:space` - that is
 * the **one-row** section, not today's: with two art-bearing rows it is **968.9px tall at 1240** with a
 * **144px** biggest run inside it @measured 2026-11-06 by `npm run check:space`. A row costs roughly what a
 * plate costs: +197px of page for the second card (D224). With these values in, the sweep re-prices `rows` at
 * 0px/0px at all six widths - the row stays as a drift guard.
 *
 * A `STATUS:CANCELLED` row is **published and not shown**: `isUpcoming` drops it from this list, while
 * `events.ics` keeps it with its UID and a raised SEQUENCE so a subscriber's client retires its copy (D223,
 * and D72's rule that a cancelled meeting is announced rather than deleted). The 2026-11-07 social is the first
 * row this happened to, and it is why the preview that showed a Nov 7 meetup no longer does.
 *
 * The file name stays `Schedule.astro` even though the compact width now carries the join band, and the
 * section's id stays `when-we-meet` even though the heading beside it now reads "Come once": both are probe
 * handles — `design/grill/rhythm-variants.mjs` and `round14-options.mjs` select the landing-page section by
 * that id. Renaming either is a separate change with its own reference sweep (the same ruling that kept
 * `WhyJoinUs.astro` named).
 */
type Props = {
  /** `compact` = the homepage's join band and a pointer at the page of record, printing no date. `full` = `/schedule`. */
  variant?: 'full' | 'compact';
  /** How many rows to render. Unset means the full variant's windowed list (`HORIZON_WEEKS`, `MAX_ROWS`, D238). */
  limit?: number;
};

const { variant = 'full', limit } = Astro.props;
const compact = variant === 'compact';

// The schedule page takes the windowed list; the homepage points at it and prints no date (D306). Both read one
// `upcoming()` call, which reads `data/events.json`, which is what `/events.ics` publishes — so the homepage's
// count of remaining dates and `/schedule`'s rows cannot drift apart.
const all = upcoming();
// `todayIn()` supplies the snapshot's own zone, so the window is measured from the same "today" the rows are
// compared against (D188: a page and its gate reading different clocks is how a date goes missing).
const win = scheduleWindow(all, todayIn());
const items = typeof limit === 'number' ? all.slice(0, limit) : win.shown;
// How many open dates the compact card is NOT showing. Counted off the same call, so the mark that says
// "there is more" cannot outrun the snapshot it came from.
const hidden = Math.max(0, all.length - items.length);
// The window adds a state the empty block has to name honestly: dates exist, and every one of them is past the
// horizon this page can show. Printing "No date is on the calendar yet" over that snapshot states a claim the
// file contradicts (D238).
const beyondWindow = items.length === 0 && win.hiddenHorizon > 0;
// What the page left out, assembled as a sentence where the counts are (D238): a window nothing mentions reads as
// "this club is quiet" rather than "this page is short". Built in the frontmatter rather than in the markup so the
// words come from the same plan that did the hiding, and so the footer stays one paragraph with one way out — the
// calendar feed — instead of two conditional clauses competing with a link for the reader's attention.
const omitted = [];
if (win.hiddenHorizon > 0) {
  omitted.push(`This page lists the next ${HORIZON_WEEKS} weeks, and ${win.hiddenHorizon} more ${win.hiddenHorizon === 1 ? 'date is' : 'dates are'} booked past ${formatDate(win.horizonEnd, 'wide')}.`);
}
if (win.hiddenCap > 0) {
  omitted.push(`It lists at most ${MAX_ROWS} dates, and ${win.hiddenCap} more ${win.hiddenCap === 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} inside that window.`);
}
const omittedText = omitted.join(' ');
const empty = content.schedule?.empty;
const venueLine = value(empty?.venueLine);
// The status word distinguishes "nothing scheduled" from "we have not wired the calendar yet" — the first
// is a fact about the club, the second is a fact about this page, and only the first may be stated as the
// club's. With no source configured the block says what the page knows: dates live on Meetup.
let status = scheduleConfigured() ? (empty?.status ?? 'Nothing scheduled') : 'Dates live on Meetup';
let emptyTitle = empty?.title ?? 'No date is on the calendar yet.';
let emptyBody = empty?.body ?? 'Dates are set in the club\'s Discord and posted on Meetup, where the calendar and the sign-up live.';
if (beyondWindow) {
  // Same notice grammar `check:render` measures (status word, lede title, body, dated line) — only the words
  // change, because the fact is different: the calendar has dates, this page just cannot show them yet.
  // `beyondWindow` cannot be true with no open date, so `firstOpen` is read through a local rather than a `!`.
  const far = win.firstOpen;
  status = 'Past this page’s window';
  emptyTitle = `The next date is ${formatDate(far?.date ?? '', 'wide')}.`;
  emptyBody = `That is more than ${HORIZON_WEEKS} weeks out, and this page lists the ${HORIZON_WEEKS} weeks it can show at once. The calendar file carries every date the organizer has made, however far out.`;
}

// The join band's copy, read from src/content.json with its source, exactly as `JoinCommunity.astro` read
// it: where meetings are held (`venues`), and what the roster says and when it was read (`membership` —
// D136: the count is dated, it has no '+' on it, and the regular-attendee figure is deliberately not
// published). The join prose around it stayed in markup, where it shipped: a new slot in content.json
// without a source would be an `openSlots` hole for `check:content` to report.
const venues = value(content.venues);
const membership = value(content.membership);
const venueCount = Array.isArray(content.venues?.names) ? content.venues.names.length : null;

// D117: a path needs a page, and it has one now, so the compact card may point at it.
const scheduleHref = '/schedule';
// When the copy was taken. This is the same stamp every published `DTSTAMP` derives from (`check:ics`'s
// dtstamp rule asserts it), so the sentence under a date and the calendar file a subscriber holds cannot
// disagree about when the club's calendar was last read.
const copied = copiedOn();
// The heading is the page's own h1 on /schedule and a section h2 on the homepage, where the hero owns the h1.
const headingId = 'when-we-meet';
const Heading = compact ? 'h2' : 'h1';
// The eyebrow is the compact width's locator. It used to read "Next date", under a card that printed one; since
// the homepage prints no date (D306) it names the section's subject instead, and the date lives one click away.
const eyebrow = 'The calendar';
// The compact lede used to be the join band's sentence, kept verbatim when the bands merged (D228): "One place to
// say you are coming, one place to argue. Neither takes a commitment, and one evening is enough to tell whether
// this is your kind of room." The operator asked for the join copy rewritten (2026-11-07), and the clause that did
// not hold up is the last one: it promises a verdict about the reader's own taste from a single evening, which is
// the page telling someone how they will feel. It also talked about the site's two surfaces ("one place ... one
// place") on a band whose job is to get someone to a table. What replaced it said the two facts this band can back
// up - where the sign-up is, and that the date above it came from the club's own calendar - and then what the thing
// being decided about actually is.
//
// That third sentence is deleted (operator ruling, 2026-11-08, in the review that also named the descriptor), and
// it is deleted here rather than reworded because the ruling is about length, not wording - the band's job is the
// two facts and the buttons. What removing it bought, measured as the round's reversal option `band-restore`
// putting the sentence back: +34px of page at 1240, +68 at 640, +101 at 390, +169 at 320, and the compact band
// going from 3 line boxes to 2 at 1240, 4 to 2 at 640, 6 to 3 at 390 and 8 to 3 at 320
// @measured 2026-11-08 by `npm run round16` (design/grill/round16/report.json). The same four numbers were
// measured from the other direction on 2026-11-07 (−34 / −68 / −101 / −169); re-measuring them against this build
// is what says the record's claim is still true after the wordmark step and the descriptor both changed. Note that
// this sentence existed only on the homepage: the full-page lede below never carried it, so `/schedule` loses
// nothing. The 2026-11-07 pricing of "math" against "mathematics" inside this clause (+28px at 1240) is history
// rather than a live trade - the noun is not on the homepage any more.
// D273 (2026-11-11): the lede no longer names a single platform. "The sign-up is on Meetup" was true of every
// printed date the day it was written, and it is the sentence a visitor carries into the row below, where the
// cluster can say Meetup, Discord, or neither (S019). A lede that names one site when the row under it offers
// two is the same defect class as a button labelled "Details and sign-up" that happens to go to Meetup. The
// claim the band can back up is the one the resolver decides per date: each printed date links to whatever
// sign-up page the club has posted for it.
const lede = compact
  // No clause about "the date above": the homepage prints none (D306, `check:dated-surfaces`). What it can say is
  // where the dates are and what a reader will find there — the same facts `/schedule`'s own lede carries, in the
  // singular, because the reader is about to follow one link.
  ? (items.length
      ? 'The next date, where the club meets, and where to sign up for it are all on the schedule.'
      : 'The club\u2019s dates come from its own calendar.')
  : 'These dates are copied from the club\u2019s own calendar. Each one links to the page the club posts its sign-up on.';
---

<section class="bg-paper py-12" aria-labelledby={headingId}>
  <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="text-center">
      {compact && <p class="text-label uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-3">{eyebrow}</p>}
      {/* Written out rather than assembled with `class:list={[...]}`: `check:classes` reads the literal word
          `list` out of that syntax and reports it as a class that generates no CSS, so the assembled form
          buys a false failure. A conditional template string keeps every token literal in the source the
          gate reads. */}
      <Heading id={headingId} class={`font-extrabold text-ink ${compact ? 'mt-1 text-heading' : 'text-display-1'}`}>{'When We Meet'}</Heading>
      <p class={`mt-4 leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body max-w-measure mx-auto ${compact ? 'text-lede' : 'text-body'}`}>{lede}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6">
      {items.length === 0 && (
        // The chosen empty state (round 7, option A) in the shipped notice grammar: status word, `--lede`
        // title, two-line body, the dated line, then the paths out. One copy of this block for both widths:
        // an empty calendar is empty on either surface, and the two paths out (Meetup, the chat) are the
        // whole answer on both. `min-h-20` is not needed: this block is prerendered at its full height, so
        // there is no arrival to shift the page.
        //
        // One boundary (operator ruling, 2026-11-03): the ring is the notice's edge and the only edge in it,
        // so the paths out sit BELOW the box rather than inside it. `role="status"` is what makes that block
        // measurable — `check:render` holds it to the notice grammar (no fill, 1px ring at the ratified
        // contrast, 48px+) and fails any filled or ringed action that ever moves back inside. Before this
        // change the primary button sat inside the ring: one box, two edges.
        <div class="max-w-measure">
          <div role="status" class="inset-shadow-rule rounded-control px-4 py-6">
            <p class="text-label font-medium text-ink-3">{status}</p>
            <h3 class="mt-1 text-lede font-bold text-ink font-body">{emptyTitle}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{emptyBody}</p>
            {venueLine && <p class="mt-2 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{venueLine}</p>}
            <p class="mt-3 text-label text-ink-3 tabular-nums">This page was built {builtOn()}{copied ? `, and the club’s calendar was last read ${copied}.` : '; no calendar source is wired to it yet.'}</p>
          </div>
          {/* The primary button belongs to the band, and the compact band has one below with the chat path
              beside it. Printing it in the notice too would put two `data-control="primary"` controls in one
              section — the gate measures each at 48px, but a band with two primaries has no primary. */}
          {!compact && (
            <div class="mt-4">
              <a
                href={`${config.URLs.meetupGroup}/join`}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                data-control="primary"
                class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 rounded-control text-label font-semibold text-band-text bg-ink hover:bg-ink-hover focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold transition"
              >
                Join on Meetup
              </a>
            </div>
          )}
          <p class="mt-3 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">
            Want to ask before you come? <a href="/chat" class="text-ink underline hover:text-gold focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold">Ask in the chat</a> —
            someone from the club reads it.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}


      {/* Two quiet links, not a paragraph. D139 keeps `data-control` for the band's one primary: a second 48px
          control under the date would compete with the date itself, which is the loudest thing in the band by
          design. `decoration-rule` keeps the underline at the hairline token rather than at the text colour,
          so it reads as the same weight as the deck above it. The count in the label is the number the deleted
          sentence used to carry; `/events.ics` keeps its path (D218) without the Google/Apple/Outlook
          sentence, which `/schedule` still says in full. */}
      {compact && items.length > 0 && (
        <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
          {hidden > 0 && (
            <a href={scheduleHref} class="text-label font-medium text-ink underline decoration-rule underline-offset-2 hover:text-gold focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold">
              {/* The label has to match what the target page actually lists: `/schedule` shows a window, not the
                  whole future, so promising "All 14 dates" on a page that prints 8 would be a link that lies
                  about its own destination (D238). */}
              {win.hiddenHorizon + win.hiddenCap > 0 ? `Next ${win.shown.length} dates` : `All ${win.shown.length} dates`} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
          )}
          <a href="/events.ics" class="text-label font-medium text-ink underline decoration-rule underline-offset-2 hover:text-gold focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold">
            Add to a calendar <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      )}

      {!compact && items.length > 0 && (
        <>
          <ul class="space-y-4" data-art-cap="323">
            {items.map((item) => <EventRow item={item} />)}
          </ul>
          {/* D218's dated line, once for the list rather than once per row: "is this current?" is asked of the
              calendar, not of a row. The stamp is the feed's own `source.fetchedAt` (`copiedOn()`), which is
              what every published `DTSTAMP` in `/events.ics` derives from \u2014 so the page and the file a
              subscriber holds cannot disagree about when the club was last read. */}
          {copied && (
            <p class="mt-3 tabular-nums"><span class="text-label text-ink-3">Copied from the club’s own calendar on {copied}</span></p>
          )}
        </>
      )}

      {/* The join band's mechanics, absorbed into this section on 2026-11-07 (D228). The tinted card is a
          FILL, not a boundary, so the section still has one keylined box (the preview card) and the page has
          one dark region (the footer). PS-3 stays named here for the same reason PS-4 stays named in the
          rows: `check:space` ranks the reserved places by `data-slot`, and `npm run round13`/`round14`
          refuse to run once the page stops naming its own photo places.
          The two paths out stand BELOW the card, on the page's own ground, and that is a measurement rather
          than a taste: the secondary is `bg-paper inset-shadow-rule`, so its ring has its own `paper` fill on
          one side and whatever it stands on on the other; inside this card it would stand on `panel`, where
          `--color-rule` measures 2.88 — under the 3.0 graphic bar (D149-3c, design/records/0018). */}
      {compact && (
        <>
          <div class="mt-8 bg-panel rounded-card p-6 sm:p-8">
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-center">
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-subhead font-semibold text-ink">How it works</h3>
                <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">
                  {venues && (
                    <p>
                      {venues}
                      {venueCount ? ` The club has used ${venueCount} of them.` : ''}
                    </p>
                  )}
                  {/* Round 17 (MS03), operator ruling 2026-11-09 (S017 B3): the band keeps only what only the
                      band can carry - the sourced venues, the venue count, the roster and its date. The
                      Meetup/chat statement lives in the hero, and the no-homework reassurance lives in the
                      hero's no-syllabus line. Removing a claim is the safe direction under check:content
                      (D92); the sentence this replaces is priced in design/grill/round17-options.mjs as
                      dup-dates-chat-band-goes and dup-homework-band-goes. */}
                  <p>
                    If you would rather hear what is being argued about before you go, the chat invite is below.
                  </p>
                  {/* D276: the roster sentence used to print "280 on the Meetup list - counted Nov 3, 2026".
                      That is a count of people, and D265 rules that the site prints dates, not attendance. The
                      slot keeps its source and its date; the number now lives only in content.json's note, and
                      `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs` reads the painted bytes for either form of tally. What a
                      visitor gets is where the list is and when it was last read. */}
                  {membership && (
                    <p>
                      {/* D306: an AGE, not the calendar date. "last read Nov 3, 2026" was the last visible date on
                          `/` after the card came off, and the 2026-11-14 audit named it. `check:dated-surfaces` now
                          refuses a date on any page it does not list, year-bearing provenance included. */}
                      {membership}, last read {content.membership.asOf ? ageSince(content.membership.asOf) : 'recently'} —
                      the list is not the room.
                    </p>
                  )}
                </div>
              </div>
              {/* PS-3, the join media column (round 10 ruling: reserve it now). Its box measured **532x56 at
                  1240** (one paragraph) inside a grid row **326** tall, and **310x85 at 390**, **240x113 at
                  320**, where the grid is one column. @measured 2026-11-03 by check:space — the paragraph that
                  used to be inside this column moved into the copy beside it, so this box needs re-measuring
                  before any round quotes it again.
                  The join path stays whether or not the plate does: art may add to the join path here, never
                  replace it (D149-1b, D94). */}
              <div data-slot="PS-3">
                <PhotoSlot
                  slot="join"
                  ratio="532 / 128"
                  caption="the club mid-session, wide crop (532x128)"
                />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
            {/* Was the page's second primary, labelled "Join on Meetup" and pointing at the group: two
                primaries on one surface and both of them Meetup is the defect D303 names. It is the same
                action as the hero's now — the club's own route, which explains the three kinds of joining —
                so it is a secondary here and the homepage keeps exactly one primary. The platform-specific
                pair that used to live here is one click further in, on /join. */}
            <a
              href="/join"
              data-control="secondary"
              class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 text-label font-semibold rounded-control text-ink bg-paper inset-shadow-rule hover:bg-tint focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold transition"
            >
              Join the club
              <svg class="ml-2 -mr-1 w-4 h-4" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true">
                <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M10.293 3.293a1 1 0 011.414 0l6 6a1 1 0 010 1.414l-6 6a1 1 0 01-1.414-1.414L14.586 11H3a1 1 0 110-2h11.586l-4.293-4.293a1 1 0 010-1.414z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
              </svg>
            </a>
            <a
              href={config.URLs.discordInvite}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              data-control="secondary"
              class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 text-label font-semibold rounded-control text-ink bg-paper inset-shadow-rule hover:bg-tint focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold transition"
            >
              Join the chat
            </a>
          </div>
        </>
      )}

      {/* One path out, in prose rather than a second button (D139 prices an in-sentence path at its 24px
          line). The link is the feed itself: a person pastes that URL into Google Calendar, Apple Calendar or
          Outlook and the dates follow the club from then on. Deliberately not a `webcal://` shortcut — an
          https link either opens a client or downloads a file a client opens, and a scheme that some browsers
          refuse would be a promise this page cannot keep. */}
      {!compact && items.length > 0 && (
        <p class="mt-6 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">
          {omittedText}
          Want them in your own calendar? <a href="/events.ics" class="text-ink underline hover:text-gold focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold">Add the club calendar</a> —
          Google, Apple and Outlook can all follow that link, and it changes when the club changes a date.
        </p>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
